=== task: mcq medium
title: Why reversing works
prompt: A stack walk that pushes the left child before the right one visits the nodes in the order node, right, left. Why does reversing that list give postorder?
* Reversing node, right, left gives left, right, node, and that holds inside every subtree too :: Right. Each block reverses into its mirror at every level.
- Because the stack walk already gives postorder :: Before the reverse, the root is first, not last.
- Because reversing a preorder list always gives postorder :: Preorder reversed gives right, left, node, which isn't postorder.
- It doesn't: the reversed list is inorder :: Inorder puts the root in the middle; the reversed list ends with the root.
